s</strong>, far fewer than the 114 that chance alone would give, and <strong>109 record highs</strong>, close to the 114 that chance alone would give. The 2010s hold the most record highs of any decade (55). Melbourne's all-time high is 102\u00b0F, on July 14, 1980.","t_chg":"Melbourne has warmed, days and nights alike","p_chg":"Across 72 complete years: Melbourne's average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.31\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.17 to +0.44), about +2.6\u00b0F since 1942. Melbourne's average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.18\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.09 to +0.27), about +1.5\u00b0F since 1942.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 8 of 12 months. Highs rose in 5 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Rain: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Melbourne's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (+0.14 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 52.1 inches a year. The wettest year was 1947 (74.28 in); the driest, 1981 (31.97 in). Melbourne almost never sees snow (the 1991\u20132020 average is 0.0 inches a winter), so there's no snowfall chart.","show_snow":false,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 9 daily record highs, 4 daily record lows"}}